\p{Hallway field note from [[conf-neuromod-nyc-2026]] — a lead, not a written-up poster. No photo.}

\subtree{\title{The work}
\taxon{remark}
\p{Some interesting work on \strong{intranasal deep-brain data collection} — using the nasal route as a shorter path toward deep structures. The open methodological question raised: how to \strong{subtract out the olfactory-bulb signal}, which sits right in the access path and contaminates whatever deeper source you are after.}
}

\subtree{\title{Our note (BCI place)}
\taxon{remark}
\p{For that access route, \strong{fiber optics look easier than EEG}: EEG's electrode/size constraints fight the geometry of a nasal path, whereas an optical fiber (fluorescence / photometry) is thin enough to go where the EEG can't and doesn't need the olfactory bulb as a return. Files against category 2 (deep-brain inference) — a cheaper, less-invasive access angle than the implanted-electrode ceiling ([[conf-motif-dot]]); the olfactory-bulb-subtraction problem is the thing to solve before it is trustworthy.}
}
